<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">United States Navy</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1775">1775</a>–</span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as US Navy</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> Navy is the naval service of the United States, founded as the Continental Navy in 1775 and re-established under the Naval Act of 1794. In the 19th century its African Squadron patrolled the West African coast to suppress the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">Atlantic slave trade</a>, and Africans freed from captured slave ships were landed in Liberia. It later became the world's largest navy and was decisive in the Pacific in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/united-states-navy.jpg" alt="United States Navy" width="320" height="321" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: United States Navy · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Emblem_of_the_United_States_Navy.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Navy originates from the Continental Navy, established in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-revolutionary-war">American Revolutionary War</a>. After the war the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> was without a navy for nearly a decade, which exposed American merchant ships to attacks by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barbary-coast">Barbary</a> pirates. The sole armed maritime presence between 1790 and the launching of the first warships in 1797 was the Revenue-Marine, the main predecessor of the Coast Guard, and its operations against the pirates were far outstripped by their depredations.</p><p>On 27 March 1794 Congress passed the Naval Act, which established a permanent standing navy and ordered the construction and manning of six frigates. By October 1797 the first three, USS United States, USS Constellation and USS Constitution, were in service. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-adams">John Adams</a>, who argued strongly for a standing navy, is often called the father of the American Navy.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/american-civil-war">American Civil War</a> the Navy blockaded the Confederacy and seized control of its rivers. A modernisation programme beginning in the 1880s, in which the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/steel">steel</a>-hulled warships stimulated the American steel industry, produced what was called the new steel navy. Its decisive victory over the outdated Spanish Navy in 1898 brought a new respect for American technical quality, and the building of pre-dreadnoughts, then dreadnoughts, brought it in line with the navies of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>. In 1907 President <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theodore-roosevelt">Theodore Roosevelt</a> ordered most of the battleships, dubbed the Great White Fleet, on a 14-month circumnavigation of the world to demonstrate the Navy's reach.</p><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a> the Navy was central in defeating the Imperial Japanese Navy in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pacific-war">Pacific War</a> and the German U-boats in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-atlantic">Battle of the Atlantic</a>, and it supplanted the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-navy">Royal Navy</a> as the world's largest navy. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cold-war">Cold War</a>, rivalled by the Soviet Navy, it began providing nuclear deterrence through ballistic missile submarines. It took a major part in the Korean and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam-war">Vietnam Wars</a> and later in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-war">Gulf War</a> and the Yugoslav Wars.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The Navy is part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">Department</a> of the Navy, alongside the coequal Marine Corps, and is headed by the Secretary of the Navy. The most senior officer is the Chief of Naval Operations, a four-star admiral who is a member of the Joint Chiefs of Staff. The uniforms were based on those of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/royal-navy">Royal Navy</a> of the time, and the predominant colours remain navy blue and white.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “United States Navy” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Alongside free-born and formerly enslaved African Americans, the colony absorbed thousands of “recaptives”, Africans freed from slave ships by the <b>United States Navy</b> and known locally as Congoes.</blockquote><a href="/empire/liberia">Liberia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In a coastal engagement on Lake Borgne, a lagoon of the Gulf of Mexico in southeastern Louisiana, the Royal Navy defeated the <b>United States Navy</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-14">14 December in history</a></li><li><blockquote>After the Battle of Roanoke Island, ships of the <b>US Navy</b>'s North Atlantic Blockading Squadron attacked the Confederate Mosquito Fleet on the Pasquotank River near Elizabeth City, North Carolina.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/february-10">10 February in history</a></li><li><blockquote>North Korean vessels captured the <b>US Navy</b> intelligence ship off Wonsan, killing one sailor.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-23">23 January in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The dying American captain James Lawrence's words, 'Don't give up the ship', became a <b>US Navy</b> motto.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-1">1 June in history</a></li><li><blockquote>It never matched the Continental Army's successes but was the predecessor of the <b>United States Navy</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/october-13">13 October in history</a></li></ul></section>
